00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:02.515
YUFENG GUO: Typically, the
goal of machine learning

00:00:02.515 --> 00:00:04.690
is to generalize
about a set of data.

00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:07.170
However, sometimes it's
possible to recover

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:10.020
information about
specific training examples

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:11.830
from a trained model.

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:14.160
How can we provide
more privacy and ensure

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:17.400
our models capture general
patterns, rather than

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:19.020
specific examples?

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:20.400
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:32.430
Welcome to AI Adventures, where
we explore the art, science,

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:34.710
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:37.290
My name is Yufeng Guo,
and on this episode,

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:40.430
we're going to take a look
at TensorFlow Privacy,

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:43.970
a new tool aimed at making
it easy to train models

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:45.740
with privacy.

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:50.180
Last year, Google published a
set of responsible AI practices

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:54.440
focused around fairness,
interpretability, privacy,

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:56.790
and security.

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:59.720
One of the recommended
practices in the Privacy section

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:04.099
states, "Appropriately safeguard
the privacy of ML models,"

00:01:04.099 --> 00:01:06.920
and recommends that we
train ML models using

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:10.460
techniques that establish
mathematical guarantees

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:12.150
for privacy.

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:14.100
This is all well and
good to recommend,

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:19.400
but it does leave you wondering,
how exactly do I do that?

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.290
When you train a
machine learning model,

00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:24.680
the goal should be to learn
these general patterns, not

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:26.870
facts about specific
training examples.

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:28.610
Otherwise the model
ends up memorizing

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:32.540
a set of specific facts,
which may not generalize well

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:35.480
to previously-unseen
data, and may

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:38.420
lead to possible exposure
of the underlying data that

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:39.900
was memorized.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:43.580
This is especially important
when that data set corresponds

00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:46.820
to user actions or
user attributes.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:50.240
Of course, it's not enough to
say that your model probably

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:52.800
doesn't memorize
specific users' data.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:54.590
What we want is
to use techniques

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:58.580
that offer strong mathematical
guarantees that models do not

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:03.880
learn or remember any details
about any specific user.

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:06.800
TensorFlow Privacy
does just this--

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:09.680
all without needing any
expertise in privacy

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:13.610
or understanding its
underlying mathematics.

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:15.980
So what's happening
under the hood?

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:19.310
In order to ensure that no
single example can impact

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:22.190
that final trained
model, TensorFlow Privacy

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:24.440
introduces some
modifications to the way

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:30.250
that gradients are calculated
during the training process.

00:02:30.250 --> 00:02:33.790
Gradients are a way of
expressing which direction

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:36.370
and how far a
model should update

00:02:36.370 --> 00:02:40.330
its internal state, represented
by weights and biases--

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:43.400
or put another way, a
large matrix of numbers.

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:46.580
And typically, during
a single training step,

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:48.280
we use a batch of data--

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:52.060
just some subset-- to
determine the gradient,

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:55.850
and then apply it to update
the value of the weights.

00:02:55.850 --> 00:02:58.790
So in other words, we look
at some bit of the data,

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:02.050
figure out, based on
that, which direction

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:06.090
and how far to shift our
model, and then we go there.

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:09.220
TensorFlow Privacy makes
a few small but key tweaks

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:11.920
to how that gradient
is computed.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:15.310
If a single example is
providing an outsize effect

00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:17.950
on that model's gradient, it
may cause it to stick out,

00:03:17.950 --> 00:03:20.290
so to speak, and
thus potentially be

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:22.750
exposed in the trained model.

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:25.990
One example of how TensorFlow
Privacy addresses this

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:30.310
is by averaging together
multiple mini-gradient updates

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:32.980
on a subset of the full batch.

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:36.610
So each mini-gradient's
value is clipped,

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:39.080
limiting its range
of possible values,

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:43.030
and so that restricts
their individual impact.

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:46.570
These mini-gradients are
then averaged together.

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:49.780
And finally, we add some
Gaussian random noise

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:51.760
on top of this average.

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:55.390
So by doing this, we're ensured
that no individual example

00:03:55.390 --> 00:03:59.880
will be memorized by the model.

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:03.360
In practice, you don't need to
modify your model architecture

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:05.910
or training procedures
to take advantage

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:08.010
of TensorFlow Privacy.

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:12.870
Instead, to train models,
use a different optimizer

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:15.270
provided by the TensorFlow
Privacy Library,

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:19.290
and we'll fill in some values
to customize the optimizer's

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:23.160
clipping, noise level,
and number of mini-batches

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:24.840
to average together.

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:27.540
Yes, we're going to introduce
some more hyperparameters

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:29.460
for you to tune,
but hey, it's better

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:32.250
than rewiring your entire
model, and it's better

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:34.420
than exposing your users' data.

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:36.690
Now let's take a look
at an example of what

00:04:36.690 --> 00:04:39.180
TensorFlow Privacy can achieve.

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:41.340
In their blog post
announcing the launch,

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:43.860
the team highlighted an
interesting situation

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:45.720
with a language
learning model which

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:48.750
is supposed to identify English
sentences that look like they

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:50.810
come from financial news.

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:52.440
With the two models
side by side,

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:55.960
one trained using TensorFlow
Privacy and one without,

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:58.680
there are many sentences
where both models agreed

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:01.230
on, say, sentences that
look like financial news,

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:05.220
such as, "South Korea and Japan
continue to be profitable."

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:09.570
But then there were the ones
which the models disagreed on.

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:12.450
These were notable, because
while the data set had marked

00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:14.550
these sentences
as financial news,

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:18.660
they definitely don't look like
they belong in financial news.

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:20.370
What's interesting
here is that not only

00:05:20.370 --> 00:05:22.650
do these anomalous
sentences account for most

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:25.890
of the differences in accuracy
between these two models,

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:27.720
but this really
illustrates the idea

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:33.440
that going by metrics purely is
not always the right decision.

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:35.590
It also suggests
that perhaps we can

00:05:35.590 --> 00:05:38.560
utilize TensorFlow
Privacy and similar tools

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:40.660
to identify aspects
of a data set

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:43.740
which perhaps shouldn't even
be there in the first place.

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:46.810
TensorFlow Privacy turns
the science and math

00:05:46.810 --> 00:05:50.350
behind differential privacy
into a tool that you can use.

00:05:50.350 --> 00:05:52.270
And while the tuning
of hyperparameters

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:54.790
does remain a bit
of an art, the team

00:05:54.790 --> 00:05:57.520
has offered up some
reasonable initial values

00:05:57.520 --> 00:05:59.920
to begin your exploration.

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:03.490
For more details and examples,
head on over to the blog post

